@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About BasketElo</PageTitle>

<div class="about-page">
    <section class="about-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">A small project with a large map</p>
        <h1>BasketElo started with a question I kept coming back to.</h1>
        <p>Could a chess idea help us see basketball differently?</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-story about-grid" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">The person behind it</p><h2 id="story-heading">An engineer, a dataset, and a lot of curiosity.</h2></div>
        <div class="about-copy"><p>I'm an engineer who has always been curious about ELO in chess: how a simple number can absorb thousands of games and still give us a changing picture of strength. I wanted to understand what would happen when that idea met basketball, where margins, home courts, leagues, seasons, and international competition add their own texture.</p><p>BasketElo began as a hobby project to compare European leagues. It was a way to learn, tinker, and answer my own questions with data rather than instinct. Since then it has grown to include the NBA and international basketball, partly because the questions kept getting more interesting—and partly because modern AI makes it dramatically easier for one person to build, test, and improve an ambitious idea.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-grid about-values" aria-labelledby="values-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">What matters here</p><h2 id="values-heading">Curiosity should leave a trail.</h2></div>
        <div class="value-list"><article><h3>Show the work</h3><p>The ratings are more useful when people can understand where they came from and question the assumptions behind them.</p></article><article><h3>Stay evidence-led</h3><p>Results are noisy. The goal is not to pretend otherwise, but to make the signal easier to explore.</p></article><article><h3>Keep learning in public</h3><p>Rulesets can improve, data coverage can expand, and the project can change its mind when the evidence earns it.</p></article></div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-closing"><div><p class="eyebrow">Why keep building it?</p><h2>Because the game is bigger than one table.</h2><p>There is something satisfying about putting different competitions on the same map while respecting their differences. BasketElo is my ongoing attempt to make that exploration clear, useful, and a little more fun.</p></div><div class="about-signoff"><span>Built as a hobby project</span><strong>with data, patience &amp; curiosity.</strong></div></section>
</div>
